  {
    "episode_id": 92298,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92298.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "that's certainly true. I think when it comes to broadband, unfortunately one thing that we have not seen change. is the access that so many folks in rural parts of our state, and to be honest and in other rural places in America, the lack of access to broadband has been a constant. We've made progress, we've made some incremental progress, but the truth is we've really got to make a lot more progress and we've got to make it fast. And the COVID situation has really, really highlighted that. You've got kids who for months couldn't go to school. If you didn't have broadband access, Wi-Fi access in your house, This was a huge detriment. I mean, how are you supposed to keep up with assignments? if you were not able to plug in and to tell the work as they say that you were at a severe disadvantage. And one positive thing about that, I suppose, is that it opened the eyes of a lot of the nation to circumstances faced by rural communities in Missouri and elsewhere every single day. And that's one of the reasons why I wrote to the chairman of the FCC Chairman Pie back in March and asked him to prioritize access to affordable broadband for rural America to put that at the top of the SEC's agenda. The truth is that we need to do a lot more. We need to make sure that there is funding, but also that there is a nationwide execution strategy to deliver affordable broadband, beginning with our underserved rural areas, and to do it quickly. And I have to tell you, I'm frustrated with the pace. I've only been here a year and a half. but I'm frustrated with the pace of the deployment and I think we've got to work harder to speed that up.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
