    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Well, the UK, I agree with you. It's absolutely key. We should look with a working class, a middle class that's growing larger and more. the Australian and other Asian nations. I mean, I think that there's there's ample opportunity. We shouldn't be shy about conducting bilateral negotiations. Listen, my view is that we should use whatever form is going to be actually good for American workers and American farmers, every form of trade deal that is. So I think the old system where you've got to do massive multilateral deals, you've got to use the auspices of the WTO, we don't have to do any of that. What we have to do is get deals that are actually good for Americans and that actually work for us that open up post markets on fair and equal terms and that are enforceable. one term. So I think that we've got to go after these new and emerging markets. I think we should use bilateral agreements wherever we can. And I think we've got to not be shy by excluding in our new agreements actual enforcement terms so that if a nation violates the term or isn't living up to our partner, then we have ways to enforce those. And it's not just to some arbitration panel at a multilateral institution where it takes years and years and we don't ever get a good result.",
